The boundary, stated precisely:

```jsx
// SERVER COMPONENT — the default (no directive needed)
async function ProductPage({ id }) {
  const product = await db.products.get(id);   // direct data access ✓
  // useState here → ERROR. window here → ERROR. onClick here → ERROR.
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>{product.name}</h1>                     {/* runs NEVER in the browser */}
      <AddToCartButton id={id} />                  {/* hand-off to client */}
    </div>
  );
}

// CLIENT COMPONENT — the opt-in directive
'use client';
function AddToCartButton({ id }) {
  const [adding, setAdding] = useState(false);   // interactivity lives HERE
  return <button onClick={() => setAdding(true)}>Add to cart</button>;
}
// AddToCartButton's code ships to the browser; ProductPage's doesn't.
```

The capability split (the interview core):

```
                     Server          Client
data fetching        direct await     via props/effects
secrets (db, keys)   ✓ safe           ✗ shipped
useState/effect      ✗               ✓
event handlers       ✗               ✓
window/localStorage  ✗               ✓
bundle cost          ZERO             JS to browser
serialize props      must be         n/a
```

The composition rules (where bugs come from):
- Server → Client: fine — pass serializable props (no functions, no class instances; plain data + Dates/Maps OK)
- Client → Server: NOT via import — a client component can't import a server one. Server code runs from client via ROUTE HANDLERS (route.ts API) or Server Actions
- 'use client' marks a BOUNDARY: everything imported below it is also client — one directive at a widget's root makes the whole widget client, which is why you place it at the leaves, not the page root

The migration instinct (the judgment layer): a page that's mostly content with small interactive islands should be ~90% server components. The anti-pattern: 'use client' on every page (App Router as Pages Router) — you pay the bundle + lose the streaming/direct-data wins for nothing.
